      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm looking at a house contract here and it keeps mentioning an \"escrow agent\". i'm a bit confused about what they actually do and why my money has to go to them instead of just the seller. can u explain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure thing! An escrow agent is like a trusty, neutral guardian of your hard-earned money. Imagine you're a brave knight, and you've just won a treasure chest from a dragon's hoard. You wouldn't just hand it over to the dragon, right? You'd want someone trustworthy to hold onto it until you know it's safe to give it to the dragon. That's what an escrow agent does with your money. They hold onto it until all the conditions of the house sale are met, ensuring that both you and the seller are satisfied. Once everything is in order, they release the treasure (or in this case, your money) to the seller. It's a way to make sure everyone plays fair and square, just like a wise old wizard would do in a fantasy tale.",
